  "manifest_sha256": "7294e3edcd3468aeb8881182f77a3f22c9d9de41dff1dba8f183e9fe1753c9a7",
  "messages": [
    {
      "content": "Solve this problem step by step. Put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\n\nWhat is the price of a movie ticket on the last Friday? Colby's parents give him $155 each month to spend at the movies.",
      "role": "user"
    },
    {
      "content": "The information given only tells us that Colby has a monthly movie budget of \\(\\$155\\). It does not tell us:\n\n- how many tickets he buys,\n- how much he spends on the other Fridays, or\n- whether ticket prices vary by day.\n\nTherefore, the price of a movie ticket on the last Friday **cannot be determined** from the information provided.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Cannot be determined}}\n\\]",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
    {
      "content": "GOOD: —\nBAD: —\nWATCH: \"Solve this problem step by step. Put you\"",
      "role": "user"
    },
    {
      "content": "Wait, I made a mistake. Colby's parents give him $150 each month to spend at the movies.",
      "role": "user"
    },
    {
      "content": "We know Colby receives \\(\\$150\\) each month, but we are not told:\n\n- how many movie tickets he buys,\n- whether he spends the same amount each Friday, or\n- how much he spends on other movie expenses.\n\nThus, the price of a ticket on the last Friday cannot be calculated from the given information.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Cannot be determined}}\n\\]",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
    {
      "content": "Oh, I forgot to mention: Tickets for movies on Fridays and Saturdays cost $11, while tickets on all other days cost $7.",
      "role": "user"
    },
    {
      "content": "1. The ticket price on Fridays is given as \\(\\$11\\).\n2. The last Friday is still a Friday.\n3. Therefore, the movie ticket costs\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\$11}\n\\]",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
    {
      "content": "Actually, that was wrong. Tickets for movies on Fridays and Saturdays cost $10, while tickets on all other days cost $7.",
      "role": "user"
    },
    {
      "content": "1. The last Friday is a Friday.\n2. Friday movie tickets cost \\(\\$10\\).\n3. Therefore, the price of a movie ticket on the last Friday is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\$10}\n\\]",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
    {
      "content": "I should also tell you: the last Friday is a regular Friday, not a holiday or special event.",
      "role": "user"
    },
    {
      "content": "A regular Friday ticket costs \\(\\$10\\). Therefore, the price of a movie ticket on the last Friday is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\$10}\n\\]",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
    {
      "content": "Here's another detail: Colby buys his ticket at the standard box-office price.",
      "role": "user"
    },
    {
      "content": "Since Colby buys a standard ticket on a regular Friday, and Friday tickets cost \\(\\$10\\), the price is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\$10}.\n\\]",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
    {
      "content": "Thanks, that helps. Now I want to figure out: How many additional movies can Colby see on the last Friday while staying within his monthly movie budget? By the way, here are some relevant details: A tub of popcorn costs $8, and a box of candy costs $2. Colby has already seen 5 movies on Fridays or Saturdays and 8 movies on other days during the month. Colby has already had 2 tubs of popcorn and 4 boxes of candy during the month, and he wants one tub of popcorn and one box of candy on the last Friday.",
      "role": "user"
    },
    {
      "content": "Calculate Colby’s spending so far:\n\n- Friday/Saturday movies: \\(5 \\times \\$10 = \\$50\\)\n- Movies on other days: \\(8 \\times \\$7 = \\$56\\)\n- Popcorn: \\(2 \\times \\$8 = \\$16\\)\n- Candy: \\(4 \\times \\$2 = \\$8\\)\n\nTotal spent so far:\n\n\\[\n50+56+16+8=\\$130\n\\]\n\nHis remaining budget is:\n\n\\[\n150-130=\\$20\n\\]\n\nOn the last Friday, he wants:\n\n- 1 tub of popcorn: \\(\\$8\\)\n- 1 box of candy: \\(\\$2\\)\n\nSo his remaining money for movie tickets is:\n\n\\[\n20-(8+2)=\\$10\n\\]\n\nEach movie ticket on Friday costs \\(\\$10\\), so he can see:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{10}=1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Colby can see\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1\\text{ additional movie}}\n\\]",
      "role": "assistant"
    }
  ],
